IMPORT prelude

§ `Directives that test against named fixtures`

-- REGRESSION EXHIBIT. R1 emits "every reachable non-exported helper" as a
-- private toplevel, and until 2026-09-21 "reachable" meant reachable from an
-- `@export`. A `#EVAL`/`#ASSERT` is emitted code too — R7 turns each one into a
-- `#[test]` scope — so a definition named only in a directive's ARGUMENTS was
-- never collected, and the directive was dropped with
--
--     directive 3 did not become a Catala `#[test]` scope:
--       `the ordinary household` is defined in this module but was not
--       collected as a helper; that is a lowering bug, please report it
--
-- which was accurate about the symptom and useless about the cause. Only
-- directives whose arguments were literals became test scopes.
--
-- That is the wrong half to keep. Naming your test data is how a rule over a
-- record with a dozen fields gets tested at all; the alternative is writing the
-- whole construction out again in every assertion, and a corpus that did so
-- would trade its fixture table for its tests. The three shapes below are the
-- ones a fixture table is made of, and each is reached ONLY from a directive:
--
--   * `the ordinary household`   — a nullary constant;
--   * `a household of`           — a unary builder, applied to a literal;
--   * `the rounded rate for`     — a helper wrapped around an exported call.
--
-- What is emitted for them is what R1 emits for any other helper: a private
-- toplevel `declaration … depends on … equals`. Nothing new is invented for
-- test data; it was simply never collected.

DECLARE Household
    HAS `adults`   IS A NUMBER
        `children` IS A NUMBER

@export The per-head allowance a household is entitled to.
GIVEN h IS A Household
GIVETH A NUMBER
`allowance for` h MEANS
    IF h's `children` GREATER THAN 0
    THEN 120 PLUS (40 TIMES h's `children`)
    ELSE 120

-- A nullary fixture. Nothing reachable from the `@export` above mentions it.
GIVETH A Household
`the ordinary household` MEANS Household WITH `adults` IS 2, `children` IS 2

-- A unary builder, so one fixture covers a family of cases.
GIVEN c IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A Household
`a household of` c MEANS Household WITH `adults` IS 2, `children` IS c

-- A helper that wraps the exported decision. It is reached only from a
-- directive, and it calls a scope — which is legal here because a `#[test]`
-- scope IS a Catala scope, and illegal from the toplevel this helper compiles
-- to, so it takes the allowance as a parameter rather than computing it.
GIVEN a IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`in tens` a MEANS a DIVIDED BY 10

#ASSERT `allowance for` `the ordinary household` EQUALS 200

#ASSERT `allowance for` (`a household of` 0) EQUALS 120

#EVAL `in tens` (`allowance for` (`a household of` 3))

-- The last two convert to nothing, and they are here for the note rather than
-- for the arithmetic: a test scope wraps a call to an `@export` decision, and
-- neither of these calls one.
--
-- Two skips, so the golden pins that BOTH are reported and that each quotes its
-- own position. The number used to count emitted TESTS rather than directives,
-- so the second skip was labelled with the first one's number — and identical
-- notes are deduplicated before emission, so it then disappeared altogether
-- instead of being reported wrongly. One directive silently untested is the
-- expensive half; the misleading number is what made it hard to see.
#EVAL 1 PLUS 1

#EVAL 2 PLUS 2
